{"review_id":"00001788303359016607-0004098830-000001-cycle-review-3","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:abf76fedf5e21a4e2ee8f5673c53672e4b8857ea6dc477993b628936925bfddb","findings":[{"finding_id":"F2-sse-utf8-chunk-split","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"SSE byte-buffer framing now holds back split multi-byte characters","claim":"The lossy per-chunk UTF-8 decode has been replaced: `stream_messages` now accumulates raw bytes in `byte_buf` and calls `drain_sse_frames`, which locates the two-byte ASCII `\\n\\n` frame terminator in the byte buffer, decodes only complete frames with `std::str::from_utf8` (propagating `AdapterError::Ambiguous` on genuinely invalid UTF-8), and leaves any trailing partial bytes — including a code point split across `bytes_stream()` chunks — in the buffer for the next chunk. A dedicated unit test (`drain_sse_frames_holds_back_a_multi_byte_character_split_across_chunks`) splits a 4-byte emoji across two chunks and asserts no frame is emitted until every byte has arrived and that the reassembled text matches verbatim. The defect described in this finding is no longer present.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/anthropic_api.rs","range":{"start":320,"end":355}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action; the byte-level framing and its split-character regression test address the original defect.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F5-unknown-stop-reason-becomes-endturn","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Unrecognized provider stop reasons are silently reported as a normal end of turn","claim":"`map_stop_reason` maps every wire `stop_reason` it does not recognize to `AdapterStopReason::EndTurn` via its `_ =>` arm, and the loop core turns `EndTurn` into `StopReason::Completed`. Anthropic's Messages API emits stop reasons beyond the six enumerated here (notably `model_context_window_exceeded`), and may add more without a client change. Any such value is therefore recorded as a successful completion rather than as an honest ceiling or failure — the inverse of the acceptance requirement that a stop must never be reported as a category it was not. The mapping table in `docs/contracts/anthropic-adapter.md` likewise enumerates only six values and does not state what happens to the rest, so the fallback's honesty cost is undocumented. No fixture drives an unrecognized `stop_reason`, so the suite cannot observe it.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":435,"end":455}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Map `model_context_window_exceeded` explicitly onto the ceiling variant, and route any remaining unrecognized-but-present `stop_reason` to a non-completing outcome (e.g. `AdapterError::Ambiguous` carrying the raw string, or an explicit unknown-stop variant) so an unmodelled provider stop is never recorded as a completion. Add a fixture that streams an unrecognized `stop_reason` and asserts the outcome is not `Completed`, and record the fallback rule in the contract's stop-reason table.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F6-wire-shape-unvalidated","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Thinking and output_config request shapes are asserted only against self-authored mocks","claim":"The adapter emits `thinking: {\"type\":\"adaptive\"}` and a top-level `output_config: {effort, format:{type:\"json_schema\", schema}}` on every reasoning/structured-output request, and `docs/contracts/anthropic-adapter.md` presents these as the current Messages API shape. Nothing in the verification package establishes that: every fixture in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/anthropic_api.rs`, `crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/anthropic.rs`, and `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/anthropic_adapter.rs` is a `wiremock::MockServer` that accepts any request body and replays a hand-authored response, so the assertions confirm only that the adapter serialized what the same author expected. If either field name or shape does not match the provider, every thinking-enabled or structured-output attempt fails with a 400 in production while the entire suite stays green. The mock-only harness is the correct choice for a no-billable-call requirement; the gap is that no independent citation (captured live response, provider schema fixture, or spec reference with a version) backs the shapes themselves.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:93c6145e22dd323d607e6347c2311452077a0299a92af60fc0f69245df1a2cda","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":97145,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-93c6145e22dd323d607e6347c2311452077a0299a92af60fc0f69245df1a2cda-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Cite the provider documentation version (or check in a captured request/response fixture) for the `thinking` and `output_config` request shapes in the contract document, and note the API version each shape belongs to alongside the pinned `anthropic-version` header, so a future reader can tell which shapes were verified against the provider and which were inferred.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F7-cancellation-fixture-vacuous","category":"test_gap","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"No fixture exercises cancellation of an in-flight submission","claim":"The acceptance criteria list cancellation among the required deterministic fixtures. The only cancellation-named test, `cancel_is_best_effort_and_never_panics`, calls `AnthropicAdapter::cancel` for an attempt id that was never submitted and asserts nothing. Every integration test in `crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/anthropic.rs` and `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/anthropic_adapter.rs` constructs a fresh `CancellationToken::new()` and never triggers it, so the loop's cancellation path — and the usage/stop-reason recorded when a stream is abandoned mid-flight — is never executed. The wall-clock timeout test covers the timeout path only, which the diff treats as a distinct case from cancellation.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":100,"end":140}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":36795,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-e5f0a1fb195249c6a2e2520fb2ba5b6500e3cdc778101c16d2b6ec4023261b4f-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Add a fixture that cancels the `CancellationToken` while a delayed mock response is in flight and asserts the resulting stop reason and that the attempt's usage is recorded ambiguous rather than zeroed or completed.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F8-adapter-local-maps-never-evicted","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"tool_use_registry and attempt_metadata grow without bound for the adapter's lifetime","claim":"`AnthropicAdapter` keeps two process-lifetime `Mutex<HashMap<..>>` caches. `tool_use_registry` gains an entry per completed `tool_use` block — holding the call name, the full parsed argument JSON, and any preceding thinking text plus signature — and is never removed, not even after the matching `tool_result` has been replayed and the turn is behind the conversation. `attempt_metadata` gains an entry per attempt and is only removed by `cancel`, which is not called on the success path. A long-lived adapter instance driving many executions therefore retains every tool argument payload and thinking transcript it has ever streamed, with no eviction policy or documented bound. The contract document notes the registry is adapter-instance-scoped but does not address its lifetime.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/anthropic.rs","range":{"start":555,"end":580}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"lint","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:bac41b1fd1ed92c0c9dc6f7f83518a35b08fa3e361159a5ad294a8d90dfd33fa","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":1268,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-59","revision":"sha256:225fe60abc5447c1e17a1910bc63bb0eb484063fe054a3af47ad28b3dfa9e6af","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-bac41b1fd1ed92c0c9dc6f7f83518a35b08fa3e361159a5ad294a8d90dfd33fa-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Give both maps a bound: evict a registry entry once its `tool_use` has been replayed into a request (or cap with an LRU keyed by insertion order), and drop `attempt_metadata` entries once the host has read them or after a fixed retention. Record the chosen bound in the contract document alongside the existing scoping note.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F9-changes-outside-allowed-paths","category":"scope_violation","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Change touches files outside the task's allowed_paths of crates/","claim":"The task declares `allowed_paths: [\"crates/\"]`, but the candidate revision also modifies `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, and `config/default.toml`, and adds `docs/contracts/anthropic-adapter.md` while modifying `docs/contracts/providers-index.md`. The workspace manifest and lockfile edits are the unavoidable consequence of the new `futures-util` dependency and the `reqwest` `stream` feature; the config and contract-document additions are commentary and normative documentation rather than code. None are listed under `prohibited_changes`, and each is defensible on its merits, but all five fall outside the declared path allowance and were not disclosed as an in-scope exception.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"config/default.toml","range":{"start":36,"end":50}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"docs/contracts/anthropic-adapter.md","range":{"start":1,"end":20}}],"remediation":"Either widen the task's `allowed_paths` to cover the workspace manifest, `config/`, and `docs/contracts/` for this PRD, or state in the submission why each out-of-scope path was required so the scope decision is on the record rather than inferred.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
